1
00:00:03,528 --> 00:00:08,815
55 million light-years away, at
the heart of the galaxy Messier 87,

2
00:00:08,815 --> 00:00:13,128
lies a black hole 6.5 billion
times the mass of our Sun.

3
00:00:13,882 --> 00:00:17,882
In 2019, astronomers revealed the
first picture of a black hole.

4
00:00:17,882 --> 00:00:23,432
This is how that picture was made, and
why a black hole looks the way it does.

5
00:00:29,731 --> 00:00:34,605
Everything you will see here was computed
from Einstein's theory of gravity,

6
00:00:34,605 --> 00:00:38,605
by tracing billions of rays of
light through curved spacetime.

7
00:00:39,344 --> 00:00:42,415
Begin with empty space,
and a field of stars.

8
00:00:42,415 --> 00:00:44,340
Now, add a black hole.

9
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:47,302
Its gravity bends the light passing by.

10
00:00:47,302 --> 00:00:52,215
Stars behind it are stretched into
arcs, duplicated, and folded into rings.

11
00:00:54,005 --> 00:00:57,155
To see why, follow
individual rays of light.

12
00:00:57,155 --> 00:01:00,893
Rays passing far from the
black hole bend only slightly.

13
00:01:00,893 --> 00:01:03,630
Closer in, they swing around sharply.

14
00:01:04,275 --> 00:01:07,623
At 1.5 times the radius
of the event horizon,

15
00:01:07,623 --> 00:01:11,448
light can travel in a circle
around the black hole.

16
00:01:11,448 --> 00:01:13,323
This is the photon sphere.

17
00:01:13,323 --> 00:01:16,823
Rays that come any closer
spiral in, and are lost.

18
00:01:17,569 --> 00:01:20,094
So a black hole casts a shadow.

19
00:01:20,094 --> 00:01:22,969
And because gravity bends
light around its edge,

20
00:01:22,969 --> 00:01:27,219
the shadow looks 2.5 times wider
than the event horizon itself.

21
00:01:28,643 --> 00:01:31,168
Real black holes are rarely dark.

22
00:01:31,168 --> 00:01:35,768
Gas spiraling inward forms a disk,
heated by friction until it glows.

23
00:01:36,384 --> 00:01:39,359
Look closely, and
something strange appears.

24
00:01:39,359 --> 00:01:44,109
We can see the far side of the disk, which
should be hidden behind the black hole.

25
00:01:44,109 --> 00:01:47,584
Its light is bent up over
the top, and down underneath.

26
00:01:48,192 --> 00:01:51,490
And hugging the shadow
is a thin, bright ring:

27
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:56,090
light that looped around the black hole,
once or more, before escaping toward us.

28
00:01:57,304 --> 00:02:00,051
But this picture is still too symmetric.

29
00:02:00,051 --> 00:02:04,101
Near the black hole, the gas orbits
at more than half the speed of light.

30
00:02:04,699 --> 00:02:08,504
On the side rushing toward
us, its light is compressed:

31
00:02:08,504 --> 00:02:09,967
bluer, and far brighter.

32
00:02:09,967 --> 00:02:12,379
On the receding side, it is stretched:

33
00:02:12,379 --> 00:02:14,204
redder, and dimmer.

34
00:02:14,204 --> 00:02:18,804
Climbing out of the black hole's gravity
robs every photon of energy, too.

35
00:02:19,365 --> 00:02:21,361
The real view is lopsided:

36
00:02:21,361 --> 00:02:24,374
one side blazing, the
other fading into the dark.

37
00:02:25,771 --> 00:02:27,834
Black holes can also spin.

38
00:02:27,834 --> 00:02:31,296
A spinning black hole drags
space itself around with it.

39
00:02:31,933 --> 00:02:35,058
Light traveling with the
spin can orbit closer in,

40
00:02:35,058 --> 00:02:38,158
while light traveling
against it is pushed away.

41
00:02:38,158 --> 00:02:40,383
The shadow is flattened on one side.

42
00:02:42,798 --> 00:02:45,185
Messier 87's black hole is a giant.

43
00:02:45,185 --> 00:02:51,222
Its event horizon is wider than the orbits
of all the planets in our solar system.

44
00:02:51,865 --> 00:02:56,689
But it is so far away that its shadow
spans just 42 microarcseconds on the sky.

45
00:02:56,689 --> 00:03:02,201
That's the size of an orange on the
surface of the Moon, seen from Earth.

46
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:07,938
The finest detail a telescope can see
is set by the wavelength it observes,

47
00:03:07,938 --> 00:03:10,538
divided by the size of its dish.

48
00:03:10,538 --> 00:03:12,413
At a wavelength of 1.3 millimeters,

49
00:03:12,413 --> 00:03:16,913
where the hot gas near the
black hole turns transparent,

50
00:03:16,913 --> 00:03:20,513
resolving that shadow would take
a dish as wide as the Earth.

51
00:03:21,221 --> 00:03:23,546
We can't build a telescope that big.

52
00:03:23,546 --> 00:03:26,071
But we can build one
that is mostly missing.

53
00:03:26,633 --> 00:03:32,555
In April 2017, radio observatories in
Hawaii, Arizona, Mexico, Chile, Spain,

54
00:03:32,555 --> 00:03:37,455
and at the South Pole, joined to
form the Event Horizon Telescope.

55
00:03:37,455 --> 00:03:44,255
Each recorded radio waves from the sky,
stamped with the time from an atomic clock.

56
00:03:44,828 --> 00:03:49,801
Petabytes of recordings were flown
on hard drives to supercomputers,

57
00:03:49,801 --> 00:03:53,026
which combined the signals
from every pair of telescopes.

58
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:56,358
Each pair measures one
ripple of the image:

59
00:03:56,358 --> 00:04:01,670
a pattern of light and dark stripes,
with a particular spacing and direction.

60
00:04:01,670 --> 00:04:05,770
Add together enough ripples, with
the right strengths and offsets,

61
00:04:05,770 --> 00:04:07,695
and you can build any image.

62
00:04:07,695 --> 00:04:10,645
That is the idea behind
the Fourier transform.

63
00:04:11,402 --> 00:04:14,588
Telescopes far apart capture fine ripples.

64
00:04:14,588 --> 00:04:17,726
Telescopes close together
capture broad ones.

65
00:04:17,726 --> 00:04:22,863
And as the Earth turns, every pair sweeps
through new spacings and directions,

66
00:04:22,863 --> 00:04:26,301
tracing arcs through the
space of all possible ripples.

67
00:04:27,244 --> 00:04:31,312
But even after a whole night,
most of that space is empty.

68
00:04:31,312 --> 00:04:35,025
Adding up only the ripples we
measured gives a "dirty image",

69
00:04:35,025 --> 00:04:37,712
smeared by the pattern
of everything we missed.

70
00:04:38,413 --> 00:04:42,175
To fill the gaps, we add what
we know about real images:

71
00:04:42,175 --> 00:04:45,525
brightness is never negative,
and the sky is mostly smooth.

72
00:04:45,525 --> 00:04:50,738
Then we search for the simplest image
that agrees with every measurement.

73
00:04:52,109 --> 00:04:54,300
And out of the gaps, a ring appears.

74
00:04:56,575 --> 00:05:02,125
Four teams, working independently on
the real data, all found the same thing:

75
00:05:02,125 --> 00:05:07,775
a ring 42 microarcseconds across, brighter
on one side, around a dark center.

76
00:05:07,775 --> 00:05:10,025
Just what Einstein's theory predicts.

77
00:05:11,636 --> 00:05:16,386
A shadow, outlined by light that skimmed
the edge of no return, and escaped.

